<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tausret</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1188 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tawosret, Twosret, Thuoris, Sitre Meryamun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tausret, also spelled Tawosret or Twosret, was the last known ruler of the Nineteenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Probably the second royal wife of Seti II, and not a king's daughter, she became regent with Chancellor Bay for his heir Siptah after Seti's death; Bay was executed on Siptah's orders in his Year 5, a move Aidan Dodson believes Tausret engineered. When Siptah died she took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, counting his nearly six years as her own; inscriptions of her Year 8 on her mortuary temple at Gournah suggest perhaps three years of sole rule. Her reign ended in a civil war, most likely with her overthrow by Setnakhte, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>, who usurped her tomb KV14.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tausret.jpg" alt="Tausret" width="330" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scan by NYPL · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tausret.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
